--------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Area                  | Simple meaning                  | Command      |
| --------------------- | ------------------------------- | ------------ |
| **Working Directory** | Where you modify code           | Edit files   |
| **Staging Area**      | Changes selected for commit     | git add      |
| **Local Repository**  | Commits stored on your computer | git commit   |
| **Remote Repository** | Repository stored on GitHub     | git push     |
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
"Git follows a four-stage workflow: Working Directory, Staging Area, Local Repository, and Remote Repository."

"First, I make changes to my source code in the Working Directory. I use git status to check which files have been modified."

"Then, I use git add to move the changes that I want to include into the Staging Area. The staging area allows me to select specific changes for my next commit."

"After that, I use git commit to save those staged changes as a commit in my Local Repository. A commit acts like a checkpoint or snapshot of my code."

"Finally, I use git push to send my local commits to the Remote Repository, such as GitHub, so that other developers can access them."

